  <Placemark>
    <name>Abenaki Indian Shop and Camp North Conway NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Abenaki Indian Shop and Camp<br/><br/>📍 Intervale, Conway, NH<br/><br/>Established in 1884 by Chief Joseph Laurent of Odanak (St. Francis, Quebec), this seasonal camp brought Abenaki basket-makers to sell handicrafts to White Mountains resort tourists — Laurent also wrote the first Abenaki-to-English dictionary, and his son Stephen continued both the craft business and the language-preservation work into the late 20th century. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1991. The Town of Conway has owned the property since 1985 and runs it as a small interpretive park.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A quiet, low-key historical stop with interpretive signage — not a shop today, despite the name.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.0742%2C%20-71.1369]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Historical</value></Data>
