    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Abraham and Maria LeFevre House Gardiner NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Abraham and Maria LeFevre House<br/><br/>📍 Forest Glen Road, Forest Glen hamlet, Gardiner, NY<br/><br/>Built in three stages between 1742 and 1798 — the nucleus constructed by Abraham LeFevre and four enslaved people, later expanded after his son John A. married his second cousin Maria LeFevre. A long, rectangular 1½-story stone dwelling under a steep gable roof, reflecting the Huguenot settlement patterns of the region. According to local tradition, an enslaved burial ground lies on the property behind the old schoolhouse. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2006.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Private property — pairs naturally with the nearby John A. Lafevre House and School, same family estate.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=41.6989%2C%20-74.1300]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
