  <Placemark>
    <name>Androscoggin Swinging Bridge Brunswick ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Androscoggin Swinging Bridge<br/><br/>📍 Union Street, Topsham / Brunswick, ME<br/><br/>A pedestrian suspension bridge spanning the Androscoggin River between Topsham and Brunswick. Built in 1892 by John A. Roebling's Sons Company — the same firm behind the Brooklyn Bridge — for just $2,000, it gave Franco-American mill workers living in Topsham Heights a safer, more direct route to the Cabot Manufacturing cotton mill in Brunswick than the existing Free Bridge. Two steel A-frame towers carry a 322-foot main span. It survived severe flood damage in 1936 and was restored and rededicated in 2007, with two descendants of John Roebling in attendance. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2004 and designated a Maine Historic Civil Engineering Landmark in 2011.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A short, easy stop — walk across for river views between Brunswick's Maine Street shops and Topsham.<br/>- Pairs well with a stop at Bath, ME or Brunswick's downtown on a day trip from Base Camp 2 Portland.]]></description>
