  <Placemark>
    <name>Arch Bridge Bristol ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Time: 2026-08-07T14:30:00<br/><br/>Arch Bridge<br/><br/>📍 Benner Road, Bristol Mills, Bristol, ME<br/><br/>A small stone arch bridge carrying Benner Road over the Pemaquid River, built from field stone, roughly quarried stone, and river cobbles laid directly on bedrock. Its exact construction date is uncertain, but circumstantial evidence points to before 1800, likely built by the Benner family — making it one of Maine's oldest stone bridges. The span is just 14 feet with a total length of 21 feet, and it remains remarkably well-preserved, having needed only modest repairs since it was built. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2003.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A quick, easy stop — pair with the lighthouse and harbor sights covered in Bristol, ME on a day trip from Base Camp 3 Camden.]]></description>
