  <Placemark>
    <name>Archibald-Adams House Cherryfield ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Archibald-Adams House<br/><br/>📍 122 Main Street (Route 193), Cherryfield, ME<br/><br/>Built around 1795, probably by Thomas Archibald, who held a number of local civic offices in the years that followed — one of Cherryfield's oldest surviving buildings. His daughter Elizabeth married Joseph Adams, who later remarried into the prominent local Campbell family after her death in 1811. Now operates as the Englishman's Bed and Breakfast. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places as part of the Cherryfield Historic District.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Combine with the nearby William M. Nash House and David W. Campbell House for a short walk through Cherryfield's historic district.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.6058%2C%20-67.9272]]></description>
