  <Placemark>
    <name>Bailey Island Cobwork Bridge Harpswell ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Bailey Island Cobwork Bridge<br/><br/>📍 Harpswell Islands Road, Bailey Island, Harpswell, ME<br/><br/>Also known as the Cribstone Bridge, this ~1,150-foot span connects Bailey Island and Orr's Island across a tidal gut called Will's Gut. Engineer Llewelyn N. Edwards of the Maine State Highway Commission designed it using roughly 10,000 tons of granite slabs stacked in an open cobwork lattice, with no mortar — a design that lets tides flow through freely instead of increasing current around the piers. It's reported to be the only granite cribstone bridge in the world. Built 1927–1928, it underwent major reconstruction in 2009–2010 using stone from the original quarry. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1975 and designated a National Historic Civil Engineering Landmark in 1984.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Stop and look down through the gaps in the granite at low tide — the open lattice construction is easiest to appreciate on foot.<br/>- Part of the scenic drive down Route 24 onto Bailey Island; combine with lobster shacks along the island.]]></description>
