  <Placemark>
    <name>Battery Steele Peaks Island ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Battery Steele<br/><br/>📍 Peaks Island, Portland, ME (via Florida Ave)<br/><br/>A 14-acre WWII coastal defense battery, completed in 1942 to protect Casco Bay from attack, now overgrown and preserved as public land by the Peaks Island Land Preserve — listed on the National Register of Historic Places. The concrete bunkers and overgrown gun emplacements give it a genuinely spooky, atmospheric feel (it's been used as a filming location).<br/><br/>Why go: An overgrown WWII bunker complex with a genuinely spooky, atmospheric vibe — a different kind of "off the beaten path" than the lighthouse crowd.<br/><br/>Who it's for: Teens/photographers/anyone who likes moody, abandoned-feeling places; not a stroller-friendly outing.<br/><br/>Likes: Real history (National Register-listed), free to explore, uncrowded, the ferry ride is part of the fun.<br/><br/>Dislikes: Requires a ferry (extra time/cost/logistics), uneven concrete with no railings, some walking/biking once on the island.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Reach it via the Casco Bay Lines ferry from Portland's Maine State Pier to Peaks Island, then it's a short bike ride or walk from the ferry landing via Florida Ave<br/>- Exploring the ruins is allowed — mind your step (uneven concrete, no railings) and pack out anything you bring in<br/>- Rent a golf cart or bike on the island if you don't want to walk the whole way<br/>- Good pairing with a broader Peaks Island afternoon — quiet streets, shoreline paths, and a slower pace than downtown Portland<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/Battery+Steele/@43.65889,-70.18056,16z]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Attraction</value></Data>
