  <Placemark>
    <name>Bear Island Lighthouse Northeast Harbor ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Bear Island Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Bear Island, Northeast Harbor, ME (Mount Desert Island)<br/><br/>Built in 1839 following a recommendation from Captain Joseph Smith to guide vessels into Mount Desert Harbor. The original wooden tower on a granite keeper's house burned in 1852 and was rebuilt as a cylindrical brick tower in 1853, with a fog bell tower added in 1887. Keeper Heber G. Sawyer rescued disabled vessels four separate times between 1914 and 1921. The light was automated and deactivated in 1981, then restored by Friends of Acadia in 1989.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Privately leased and closed to the public — best seen by boat or seaplane from Northeast Harbor.<br/>- Bar Harbor Whale Watch and other lighthouse cruises pass close by.]]></description>
