    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Belfast and Searsport Day Trip</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Belfast & Searsport Day Trip<br/><br/>📍 Belfast & Searsport, ME (Belfast: ~18 miles, 25 min from Camden; Searsport: ~10 min farther north)<br/><br/>A full day exploring maritime history, antiques, and authentic small-town waterfront character. Both towns draw locals rather than cruise-ship tourists, offering genuine Maine character distinct from the busier coastal alternatives. Pairs perfectly with your Augusta/Hallowell trip to offer two completely different day experiences from Camden base.<br/><br/>Itinerary<br/><br/>Late Morning: Belfast Waterfront Exploration (10:00 a.m.–11:30 a.m.)<br/>Start in downtown Belfast, exploring the working waterfront with its historic brick storefronts, locally run shops, and cafés. Frequently described as having more authentic character than busier coastal stops.<br/><br/>Points of interest:<br/>- Working waterfront docks and views<br/>- Historic Main Street brick architecture<br/>- Local independent shops and galleries<br/>- Casual cafés and coffee shops<br/>- No cruise-ship tour buses (unlike Bar Harbor)<br/><br/>Late Morning/Midday: Penobscot Marine Museum (11:45 a.m.–1:15 p.m.)<br/>Continue about ten minutes north to Searsport and spend time at the Penobscot Marine Museum, one of the oldest maritime museums in the country. Showcases Maine's shipbuilding and seafaring history through ship models, paintings, and artifacts from the age of sail—a deep dive into maritime heritage that connects to the working waterfront you saw in Belfast.<br/><br/>Museum highlights:<br/>- Historic ship models and paintings<br/>- Age of sail artifacts and documents<br/>- Shipbuilding history exhibitions<br/>- Campus-based with multiple buildings<br/>- Plan 1–2 hours for a full visit<br/><br/>Afternoon: Searsport Antiques (1:30–3:00 p.m.)<br/>Browse Searsport's Route 1 corridor, regionally known as an antiques destination with several shops worth a slow browse. Connects to maritime history theme—many shops feature nautical and vintage maritime items.<br/><br/>Late Afternoon: Return to Belfast Dining (3:30–5:00 p.m.)<br/>Return to Belfast for a relaxed lobster or seafood lunch before the short 25-minute drive back to Camden. Waterfront dining with views, local seafood focus, and genuine small-town character.<br/><br/>Why This Trip Works<br/><br/>- Drive time: Belfast is only 25 minutes from Camden—practically a warm-up drive<br/>- Theme differentiation: Maritime heritage and small-town character, completely distinct from Augusta/Hallowell's state capital focus<br/>- Authenticity: Both towns frequently described as having more authentic local character than touristy alternatives<br/>- No overlap: Routes south/southwest on Route 1, not competing with Bar Harbor routes<br/>- Variety: Mix of working waterfront, museum history, antiques, and casual waterfront dining<br/>- Proximity: Both towns are close together, making for an efficient full-day outing<br/>- No crowds: Locals rather than cruise-ship tourists, genuine Maine character<br/><br/>Notes<br/><br/>- Both towns draw primarily local and regional visitors, not cruise-ship traffic<br/>- Penobscot Marine Museum requires admission (check hours)<br/>- Waterfront dining is the highlight of the afternoon—take your time with the scenic views<br/>- Good for history lovers and antique collectors<br/>- The working waterfront vibe is the key distinguishing feature vs. more developed tourist destinations<br/>- Best as a full-day outing to appreciate both towns and the maritime museum]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-orange</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>day-trip</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>gem</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>orange</value></Data>
