  <Placemark>
    <name>Blue Hill Bay Lighthouse Brooklin ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Blue Hill Bay Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Near Brooklin, ME (Blue Hill Bay)<br/><br/>Built in 1856 — a cylindrical white tower attached to a one-and-a-half story dwelling — to guide lumber ship traffic when nearby Ellsworth was the world's second-busiest lumber port. Keeper Roscoe Chandler assisted vessels in the fog and, during Prohibition, once aided a grounded ship carrying contraband. Deactivated in 1934 and replaced by automated lights.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Private property, no public access; best viewed by boat or plane, or by wading at low tide (risky, not recommended).<br/>- Isle au Haut Boat Services cruises pass the lighthouse.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
