  <Placemark>
    <name>Boothbay Railway Village Boothbay Harbor ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Boothbay Railway Village<br/><br/>📍 586 Wiscasset Road (Route 27), Boothbay, ME<br/><br/>A recreated early-1900s Maine village spread across 35 acres, built from real historic structures relocated from around the region — shops, a schoolhouse, a homestead, and the 1912 Freeport Station depot. The centerpiece is a ride behind a 100-year-old vintage steam locomotive around the grounds; there's also an antique auto museum with 60+ vehicles spanning 1902–1962, and a detailed model railroad depicting 1950s–60s Maine railroading. The resident goats are a running highlight for younger visitors.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Train rides depart on the hour, 11am–4pm, Tuesday–Sunday — call ahead ((207) 633-4727) to confirm the schedule since it can shift seasonally.<br/>- About 6 miles north of Boothbay Harbor proper on Route 27 — plan it as its own outing, not a quick add-on to a harbor walk.<br/>- Guided 1.5-hour tours include the train ride; worth it for real context on the buildings and autos rather than just wandering.<br/>- Great option if traveling with kids — hands-on, unhurried, and the goats alone are worth the stop.<br/>- Iron Horse Antiques & General Store on-site is worth a browse before or after.]]></description>
