  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Burnt Island Lighthouse Boothbay Harbor ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Burnt Island Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Burnt Island, Boothbay Harbor, ME<br/><br/>Completed in 1821 on a five-acre island purchased for $150, this thirty-foot conical granite tower is Maine's ninth lighthouse and the second-oldest original lighthouse structure in the state, after Portland Head (1791). Staffed until automation in 1988; today the Maine Department of Marine Resources runs it as a "Living Lighthouse," with costumed interpreters portraying the keeper's family and demonstrating 1950s-era lighthouse life.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Only reachable by boat — book the Burnt Island Living Lighthouse Tour through Balmy Days Cruises (balmydayscruises.com, (207) 633-2284), departing Pier 8 in Boothbay Harbor. Adults $34, children ages 3–11 $24.<br/>- Island open 10am–5pm daily, May 15–October 15 — has to fall within that window to actually land and tour the grounds.<br/>- Adventurous paddlers do sometimes kayak out independently, but the boat tour is the practical way to actually go ashore.<br/>- If you don't book the boat tour, it's still viewable at a distance from shore.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
