  <Placemark>
    <name>Camden Public Library Amphitheatre and Fawn's Garden Camden ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Camden Public Library Amphitheatre & Fawn's Garden<br/><br/>📍 Camden Public Library, 55 Main St, Camden, ME<br/><br/>A horseshoe-shaped stone amphitheatre built 1928–1931, designed by celebrated American landscape architect Fletcher Steele and funded by local arts patron Mary Louise Curtis Bok. Massive boulders, Camperdown elms, and stands of birches give it a naturalistic feel despite every element being deliberately planned. Sits right on the library grounds with a real view of Camden Harbor, and offers a genuinely quiet place to sit away from downtown traffic.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Free and open to the public year-round; the library itself is a National Historic Landmark.<br/>- Look for the large brass-ringed compass set into the ground near the Faun's Garden — its ring famously went missing for over 50 years before turning up in a nearby shed.<br/>- Used regularly for weddings, yoga, and community events — worth checking there's nothing scheduled if you want a quiet visit.<br/>- Benches are placed to catch the harbor view — a good spot to sit for 10–15 minutes between other stops rather than a destination in itself.]]></description>
