  <Placemark>
    <name>Cape Elizabeth Lighthouse Cape Elizabeth ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Cape Elizabeth Lighthouse (Two Lights, East Tower)<br/><br/>📍 Two Lights Road, Cape Elizabeth, ME<br/><br/>One of the "Two Lights" twin towers built in 1828 after Portland Harbor became a major port; rebuilt in cast iron in 1873–75. Keeper Marcus Aurelius Hanna earned the Gold Lifesaving Medal in 1885 for rescuing two sailors from the wrecked schooner Australia. Painter Edward Hopper immortalized the lights between 1927 and 1929, and one of those paintings later appeared on a 1970 U.S. postage stamp. The tower is leased to the American Lighthouse Foundation; the dwelling remains private.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Best public view is from The Lobster Shack Restaurant at the end of Two Lights Road, right next to the fog signal building.<br/>- Pair with the nearby west tower (Cape Elizabeth West Lighthouse), the inactive twin.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
