  <Placemark>
    <name>Carriage Roads Acadia National Park ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Carriage Roads<br/><br/>📍 Acadia National Park ME<br/><br/>45 miles of crushed-stone carriage roads built by John D. Rockefeller Jr. between 1913 and 1940, specifically to keep automobiles out of the park — a ban he personally enforced for decades. No motorized vehicles ever. The roads wind through forest, across 17 hand-built granite bridge arches of different designs, past Eagle Lake, Jordan Pond, and Bubble Pond. Biking these roads is the single most distinctive experience Acadia offers.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Rent bikes in Bar Harbor at Acadia Bike or Bar Harbor Bicycle Shop — all have carriage road maps.<br/>- The Eagle Lake loop (around the lake) is 5.8 miles and suitable for all abilities.<br/>- The network of bridges is a marvel — each one different; Rockefeller insisted no two be alike.<br/>- Start early; the roads near Jordan Pond get crowded by mid-morning in peak season.<br/>- The carriage roads are also excellent for walking — the surface is smooth and packed.]]></description>
