  <Placemark>
    <name>Curtis Island Lighthouse Camden ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Curtis Island Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Curtis Island, Camden Harbor, ME<br/><br/>Built in 1836 on what was originally called Negro Island, renamed in 1934 for publishing magnate Cyrus Curtis. The original rubblestone tower cracked in an 1842 gale and was replaced by the current brick tower in 1896. Site of two notable rescues: keeper's daughter Louise Gilley saved two capsized boys in 1897, and keeper Francis McCarthy rescued a downed Air Force pilot in 1962.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Grounds are open, dwelling and tower closed. Reaching the island requires a boat — kayak, private boat, charter, or a rentable rowboat (about $40/day); it's roughly a half-hour row in calm conditions.<br/>- Easiest option: any Camden windjammer day sail (see Windjammer Cruises) passes right by the light heading out into Penobscot Bay.<br/>- For a land view without a boat: take Bay View Street toward Rockport, and at about 0.8 miles look for a small unmarked path across from Beacon Avenue — a short walk leads to a bench with one of the best on-land views of the island (see Curtis Island Overlook).]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
