  <Placemark>
    <name>Damariscotta and Pemaquid Peninsula Day Trip</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Damariscotta & Pemaquid Peninsula Day Trip<br/><br/>📍 Damariscotta/Newcastle & the Pemaquid Peninsula, ME (~40-55 min from Camden)<br/><br/>A full-day loop covering both the Damariscotta "Twin Villages" area and the Pemaquid Peninsula (New Harbor, Pemaquid Point, Round Pond). The whole route has only one real crowd bottleneck — Pemaquid Point Lighthouse — so the entire itinerary is built around hitting that stop first, then spending the rest of the day in villages and preserves that stay quiet even in peak season.<br/><br/>Crowd-Avoidance Strategy<br/><br/>- Pemaquid Point Lighthouse is the only genuinely crowded stop on this whole route (~100,000 visitors/year through a small lot). Everything else — Round Pond, the nature preserves, even downtown Damariscotta — stays comfortably uncrowded by comparison.<br/>- Do the lighthouse first, before 9-10am, then work outward. By the time it's mobbed (usually mid-morning through mid-afternoon in summer), you'll already be somewhere quiet.<br/>- Parking notes are on the map for the three spots along this route where parking is worth planning for: Pemaquid Point Lighthouse, Downtown Damariscotta, and Round Pond Harbor (this last one mostly just to mark where the walkable center is — Round Pond itself is rarely crowded).<br/><br/>Itinerary<br/><br/>Early Morning: Pemaquid Point (8:30-10:00am)<br/>Drive straight to the tip of the peninsula (~55 min from Camden) and arrive at opening or as close to it as you can manage.<br/><br/>- Pemaquid Point Lighthouse (4.8★) — climb the tower, walk the granite ledges, visit the Fishermen's Museum in the keeper's house, before the lot fills and buses arrive. Park at the on-site lot.<br/>- Pemaquid Beach Park (4.8★, 5 min away) — a short beach walk while it's still cool and quiet, before midday beachgoers show up.<br/><br/>Late Morning: Colonial History (10:15-11:30am)<br/>- Colonial Pemaquid State Historic Site + Fort William Henry (4.8★) — a reconstructed 1907 fort on its original 17th-century foundation, with a small artifacts museum. Stays quiet even at midday.<br/>- Rachel Carson Salt Pond Preserve — easy 1.1-mile trail past tide pools; check tide times before you go.<br/><br/>Midday: New Harbor & Round Pond (11:45am-2:00pm)<br/>- Quick stop for Harbor Ice Cream or lunch at The Contented Sole (wharf-side seating over the harbor) in New Harbor.<br/>- Drive ~10-15 min north to Round Pond — a genuinely quiet working harbor village even in August. Lunch at Round Pond Lobster Co-op or Muscongus Bay Lobster (both right on the water), browse Granite Hall Store (4.9★) for penny candy and gifts, and King Ro Market for a general-store browse. Park near the town landing — everything is a short walk.<br/><br/>Early Afternoon: Quiet Nature Detour (2:00-3:15pm)<br/>If you still have energy, these are genuinely uncrowded and worth the short detours:<br/>- LaVerna Preserve (4.9★) — coastal cliff trail near Round Pond.<br/>- Arch Bridge (5.0★) — a pre-1800 stone bridge, one of Maine's oldest.<br/>- Todd Wildlife Sanctuary (5.0★) — Audubon-run birding refuge.<br/><br/>Late Afternoon: Damariscotta/Newcastle (3:30-5:30pm)<br/>Drive north into the Twin Villages (~20 min from Round Pond).<br/><br/>- Whaleback Shell Midden — a quiet historic stop on the way in.<br/>- Downtown Damariscotta Main Street — browse Skidompha Secondhand Book Shop, coffee/pastry at Barn Door Baking Co or Ann's Book Bistro (4.7★). Park at the First National Bank lot — everything's a short walk from there.<br/>- Optional wind-down: Oxbow Brewing Company — a low-key rural farmhouse brewpub, never crowded.<br/><br/>Return to Camden (~45 min)<br/><br/>Why This Route Works<br/><br/>- One bottleneck, hit first: Pemaquid Point is the only stop on this whole loop with real crowd pressure — going there before 10am neutralizes the worst of it.<br/>- Round Pond over New Harbor for lunch: New Harbor's restaurant row (Contented Sole, Bradley Inn) gets busier at midday; Round Pond stays a working village even in peak season.<br/>- Nature preserves as a buffer: LaVerna, Rachel Carson, Arch Bridge, and Todd Wildlife Sanctuary are all essentially never crowded — good places to spend the busiest midday hours while Pemaquid Point and the beach fill up behind you.<br/>- Damariscotta saved for late afternoon: downtown has real parking capacity (60+ spaces at the First National Bank lot) and doesn't get the same morning rush as the peninsula.<br/><br/>Notes<br/><br/>- This is a full day if you do all of it — if that's too much, split it: Peninsula loop (Pemaquid Point through Round Pond) as one day, Damariscotta/Newcastle as a separate, shorter trip.<br/>- Best in shoulder season (fall) if crowd avoidance matters more than warm-beach weather — Pemaquid Point in particular thins out noticeably after Labor Day.<br/>- Check tide times before Rachel Carson Salt Pond Preserve — it's built around tide pools.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-orange</styleUrl>
