    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Dixville Notch State Park Colebrook NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Dixville Notch State Park<br/><br/>📍 Route 26, Dixville, NH (12 miles east of Route 3)<br/><br/>Home to Table Rock, a steep but short climb (sources vary: 1.5 miles/700 ft gain to 3 miles/1,100 ft gain) to one of the most striking — and unnerving — overlooks in New Hampshire. The summit is a narrow ledge full of crevices never more than six feet wide, with a sheer 700-foot drop on every side, looking straight down at the former Balsams Resort and Lake Gloriette, plus Mt. Gloriette and the abandoned Balsams Wilderness Ski Area beyond.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Trailhead and parking are on Route 26, marked by a Dixville Notch Wayside sign, 12 miles east of Route 3.<br/>- Not for those with a fear of heights or exposed drops — Table Rock's perch is genuinely unnerving even for confident hikers.<br/>- Roughly 45 minutes to an hour round trip at a steady pace.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.8625%2C%20-71.2917]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Attraction</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>mountain</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>purple</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-71.2917,44.8625,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Dodge Point Preserve Newcastle ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Time: 2026-08-10T11:30:00<br/><br/>Dodge Point Preserve<br/><br/>📍 River Road, Newcastle, ME (main parking lot ~2.5 mi from US-1 on River Road, left side)<br/><br/>A 500+ acre Public Reserved Land parcel on the Damariscotta River, with over 8,000 feet of river frontage — forest, old stone walls, ponds, and both sandy and pebble beaches. Trails are generally wide, well marked, and mostly flat to gently rolling.<br/><br/>Recommended route<br/>Old Farm Road Trail → Shore Trail, out and back — an easy ~3.3-mile loop, roughly 1h45m at a relaxed pace, with trail maps posted at major intersections. For a shorter outing, walk the Old Farm Road out to the Shore Trail, spend time at one of the small beaches, then turn back whenever.<br/><br/>Trail Options<br/>- Old Farm Road Trail — 2 mi loop, easy, wide former farm/logging road, gentle grades, woods and stone walls<br/>- Shore Trail — 1.5 mi, easy, the prettiest option — follows the river to sandy and pebble beaches<br/>- Timber Trail — 0.8 mi, easy, short woodland connector through mixed forest and stone walls<br/>- Ravine Trail — 1.2 mi, moderate (steeper and more uneven than the rest) — skip this one if you want an entirely gentle walk<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Use the main Dodge Point parking lot on River Road, not the River-Link lot — the connector from River-Link is steep and rocky<br/>- Dogs allowed, leashed required weekends 9am-4pm<br/>- Can be muddy after rain — hiking shoes useful, though not a heavy-boot hike<br/>- Right next to Damariscotta/Newcastle — easy to fit in before or after Main Street]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
