  <Placemark>
    <name>Eagle Island Lighthouse Deer Isle ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Eagle Island Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Eagle Island, near Deer Isle, ME<br/><br/>Congress funded this lighthouse in 1837 to guide lumber ships bound for Bangor during Maine's logging boom; the light was activated September 28, 1838. The 25-foot rubblestone tower got a fourth-order Fresnel lens in 1857. Keepers here rowed two miles for supplies and climbed steep trails to the station — conditions stayed primitive well into the 20th century. Automated in 1959, now solar-powered, and under multi-phase restoration since 2017.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Grounds open, tower closed. Eagle Island Mailboat runs seasonal service from Sunset on Deer Isle.<br/>- Eagle Light Caretakers hold an annual open house each August — worth checking dates against the trip window.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
