    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Faulkner Park Trailhead Woodstock VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Faulkner Park Trailhead<br/><br/>📍 Mountain Avenue, Woodstock, VT<br/><br/>Built in 1937 by Marianne Faulkner in memory of her husband Edward, who suffered from crippling rheumatoid arthritis — she modeled the gentle, gradual switchbacks on "cardiac health" trails the couple had visited in Baden-Baden, Germany. The trail climbs Mount Tom (1,357 ft, part of Marsh-Billings-Rockefeller National Historical Park) through sugar maples, hemlocks, and evergreens, with benches along the way. The last 100 yards to the South Peak — added later, not part of Faulkner's original design — is steeper and rockier, with handrails in places. About 3.9 miles round trip with 675 ft of gain (1.5-2 hours), ending at a panoramic overlook of Woodstock village.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A genuinely gentle climb for most of its length — good for those wanting a real summit view without a strenuous hike.<br/>- Dogs welcome on leash.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.6233%2C%20-72.5260]]></description>
