  <Placemark>
    <name>Fernald's Neck Preserve Lincolnville ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Fernald's Neck Preserve<br/><br/>📍 Fernalds Neck Road, Lincolnville ME (on Megunticook Lake)<br/><br/>A 325-acre peninsula preserve in the middle of Megunticook Lake, mostly mature hardwoods, hemlocks, and pines. Three trails of varying length and difficulty, with shoreline swimming access via Balance Rock on the Orange/White trails. No dogs or hunting permitted. Originally donated to The Nature Conservancy in 1969, transferred to Coastal Mountains Land Trust in 2007.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- White Trail (0.4mi, easy), Orange Trail (1.75mi, moderate, includes Balance Rock swimming access), Blue Trail (1.8mi, moderately difficult) — pick based on how much time/effort you want.<br/>- Parking lot holds only 11-12 cars, gate open 6:30am-7:30pm — arrive early in peak season.<br/>- This is the destination note's own namesake attraction — previously had no Place note at all.<br/>- Coordinates above are an approximation — worth confirming the exact parking-lot location against Google Maps before you go.<br/><br/>Fernald’s Neck Preserve is a beautiful, quiet, lake-centered woodland hike near Camden—but it is not as effortless as Beech Hill or Merryspring. Go for mature pines, swimming spots, Balance Rock, and a genuinely wild-feeling peninsula on Megunticook Lake; expect roots, rocks, a few short steep sections, and no dogs.[1][2][3]<br/><br/>At a glance<br/><br/>- Location: Lincolnville, about 15 minutes north of Camden via Route 52/Mountain Street. From Camden, drive about 5.8 miles north on Route 52, turn left onto Fernalds Neck Road, stay left at the fork, and continue to the parking lot.[2][1]<br/>- Size: 285 acres covering most of a peninsula extending into Megunticook Lake.[1][2]<br/>- Trail network: About 3.8–4 miles total, with roughly 3 miles of protected shoreline.[3][4][2]<br/>- Best features: Mature pine/hemlock forest, lake shore, swimming access, Great Bog wildlife habitat, rocky bluffs, and Balance Rock.[2][1]<br/>- Dogs: Not allowed, except service animals; a few third-party listings are outdated or incorrect.[5][6][1]<br/><br/>The experience<br/><br/>The preserve feels notably more remote and forested than its distance from Camden suggests. The woods are dominated by big pines, hemlocks, and hardwoods, creating a cool, shaded understory with mossy rocks, fungi, and intermittent lake views.[1][2]<br/><br/>This is not a continuous shoreline promenade. You travel through the woods, then reach selected lake-access points, bluffs, and small rocky swimming spots—so it rewards people who take the network rather than simply walking to one viewpoint and leaving.[3][2]<br/><br/>What not to miss<br/><br/>Balance Rock<br/><br/>Balance Rock is a huge glacial erratic perched apparently precariously on bedrock. It is close to the trailhead—about 0.2 mile with only 19 feet of elevation gain—so it is an easy, worthwhile stop even if you do not do the full hike.[7][1]<br/><br/>Blue Trail bluff view<br/><br/>The Blue Trail has the preserve’s strongest scenic viewpoint: a high bluff with a broad lake view and a backdrop toward Mount Megunticook. The far end of this trail is one of the rockier and hillier parts of the network, but the view is the preserve’s best “high reward” payoff.[2][3]<br/><br/>Orange Trail lake access<br/><br/>The Orange Trail leads to shoreline access and a small beachy/rocky swimming area. It also includes Balance Rock, but it is among the more uneven and hilly routes, so wear proper walking shoes rather than sandals.[8][1][2]<br/><br/>Great Bog<br/><br/>Where the Blue and Orange trails run near Great Bog is the best zone for birding and wildlife watching. Watch the lake and shoreline for bald eagles and other water-dependent wildlife.[2]<br/><br/>White Trail lake access<br/><br/>The short White Trail is the easiest route: 0.4 mile round trip. Use it for a quick, low-effort route to the lake, especially if you want to swim or simply sit at the water rather than undertake the more rugged loop.[1]<br/><br/>Trail choices<br/><br/>| Route | Distance | Difficulty | Best for |<br/>|---|---:|---|---|<br/>| Balance Rock spur | ~0.2 mi | Easy | Quick landmark stop, minimal effort [7] |<br/>| White Trail | 0.4 mi round trip | Easy | Fastest route to lake access [1] |<br/>| Blue Trail | 1.8 mi round trip | Moderate | Best bluff view and a satisfying shorter hike [1][3] |<br/>| Orange Trail | 1.75 mi round trip | Moderate | Swimming access, rocky shoreline, Balance Rock [1][2] |<br/>| Main loop / Blue + Orange combination | ~3.5–4 mi | Easy-moderate | The full experience: forest, bog, lake, bluff, and swimming spots [2][8] |<br/><br/>How hard is it?<br/><br/>The preserve is officially a mix of easy and moderate routes, and that is accurate. Its main challenge is footing, not sustained elevation: exposed roots, rocks, and a couple of short climbs to bluffs.[9][3][1]<br/><br/>A common 1.9-mile loop is rated easy with about 98 feet of gain, but the Blue and Orange Trail outer sections become rougher and hillier. If you are comfortable with natural forest paths and can manage uneven ground, it is very manageable; if you want paved, flat, or obstacle-free walking, choose Round Top Farm, Salt Bay Farm, or Merryspring instead.[10][11][12][2]<br/><br/>Best route for you<br/><br/>The complete but sensible outing<br/><br/>Allow 1.5 to 2 hours and take the Blue Trail toward the scenic bluff, then use the Orange Trail to reach the lake and swimming area, stopping at Balance Rock near the beginning or end. This gives you every major payoff without trying to turn the outing into a long-distance hike.[3][2]<br/><br/>The lighter version<br/><br/>Walk to Balance Rock, take the short White Trail to the lake, then return. It is the best choice if you want quiet forest and water scenery with the least physical effort.[7][1]<br/><br/>Practical details<br/><br/>- Parking: Limited—roughly 11–12 vehicles—at the end of a narrow, winding dirt road. Park only in the designated lot, not along the road.[3][1]<br/>- Gate: Listed as opening at 6:30 AM and closing promptly at 7:30 PM. Do not risk returning late to a locked gate.[1][2]<br/>- Footwear: Trail shoes or hiking shoes are advisable; skip flip-flops, especially on the Blue and Orange trails.[8][3]<br/>- Swimming: Bring water shoes or sturdy sandals if you plan to enter the lake—the shore access is rocky rather than a groomed beach.[3][1]<br/>- Cell coverage / facilities: Treat it as a simple backcountry-style preserve: no facilities or staffed visitor center are listed, so bring water, insect repellent, and a downloaded map.[2][1]<br/>- Check before driving: Coastal Mountains Land Trust notes that unpaved-road access and trail conditions can lead to temporary closures, particularly during mud season.[4]<br/><br/>Bottom line<br/><br/>Fernald’s Neck is best when you want a more immersive, uncrowded, wooded-and-water experience than a pure scenic overlook. It is one of the better hidden-gem walks near Camden, but choose it for natural forest, lake swimming, and a moderate amount of uneven footing—not for a completely easy stroll.[2][3]<br/><br/>Sources<br/>[1] Fernalds Neck Preserve - Coastal Mountains Land Trust https://www.coastalmountains.org/preserves/fernalds-neck-preserve/<br/>[2] Fernald's Neck Trails - Maine Trail Finder https://www.mainetrailfinder.com/trails/trail/fernalds-neck-trails<br/>[3] Fernald's Neck Preserve — Maine by Foot https://mainebyfoot.com/fernalds-neck-preserve-lincolnville/<br/>[4] maps - Coastal Mountains Land Trust https://www.coastalmountains.org/maps/<br/>[5] Lincolnville - Maine by Foot https://mainebyfoot.com/lincolnville/<br/>[6] 7 Maine hiking trails where dogs aren't allowed - Piscataquis Observer https://observer-me.com/2022/09/04/living/7-maine-hiking-trails-where-dogs-arent-allowed/<br/>[7] Balancing Rock Trail | Hiking Trail Maps, Difficulty, Trail Status | onX https://www.onxmaps.com/hiking/m3qxy47n7609/balancing-rock-trail<br/>[8] Fernald's Neck Preserve - All You SHOULD Know Before Going ... https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g40714-d3507752-Reviews-or10-Fernald_s_Neck_Preserve-Lincolnville_Maine.<br/>[9] Fernald's Neck Preserve - All You SHOULD Know Before Going ... https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g40714-d3507752-Reviews-Fernald_s_Neck_Preserve-Lincolnville_Maine.<br/>[10] Fernalds Neck, Maine - 222 Reviews, Map | AllTrails https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/maine/fernalds-neck<br/>[11] Round Top Farm - Coastal Rivers Conservation Trust https://www.coastalrivers.org/trails/round-top-farm/<br/>[12] Salt Bay Farm - Coastal Rivers Conservation Trust https://www.coastalrivers.org/trails/salt-bay-farm/<br/>[13] Hiking and swimming at Fernald's Neck Preserve in Camden, ME https://www.facebook.com/groups/BayAreaHike/posts/3556976557804760/<br/>[14] Hiking Fernald's Neck Preserve in Lincolnville Maine - Facebook https://www.facebook.com/groups/769793960315085/posts/1728885631072575/<br/>[15] The Most Dog-Friendly Hikes and Walks in Midcoast Maine This ... https://dwellinginmaine.com/blog/posts/2025/06/12/paws-paths-the-most-dog-friendly-hikes-and-walks-in-midcoast-maine-this-summer/<br/>[16] FERNALD'S NECK PRESERVE - Updated July 2026 - Parks - - Yelp https://www.yelp.com/biz/fernalds-neck-preserve-lincolnville<br/>[17] Looking for great hikes to do with your dog? A new guide offers 45 ... https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OC7JuvAgX2Y]]></description>
