  <Placemark>
    <name>Fort Williams Park Cape Elizabeth ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Fort Williams Park<br/><br/>📍 1000 Shore Rd, Cape Elizabeth, ME<br/><br/>A sprawling oceanfront park built on the grounds of a decommissioned Army post active through both World Wars — walkable concrete bunkers and gun emplacements sit alongside dramatic cliffs, open lawns, and Maine's most photographed lighthouse, Portland Head Light. Free to enter (parking fee applies), open sunrise to sunset.<br/><br/>Why go: Portland's most-photographed lighthouse plus walkable fort ruins on dramatic ocean cliffs — history and scenery in one stop.<br/><br/>Who it's for: Everyone — families, dog owners, photographers, history buffs. Genuinely broad appeal.<br/><br/>Likes: The lighthouse-and-cliffs view, ruins/bunkers kids can climb around, food trucks on-site, free entry.<br/><br/>Dislikes: Parking fills up fast in summer; can feel busy/touristy midday.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Walk the sea trail past the old armaments and overlooks — the ruins photograph especially well against the cliffs and crashing surf on a day with decent swell<br/>- Portland Head Light Museum & Gift Shop and the Cape Elizabeth Historical Society Museum are both on-site<br/>- Food trucks (including Bite Into Maine's lobster roll van) are regularly parked in the lot<br/>- Wheelchair accessible, dog-friendly (off-leash area available), good for kids — playgrounds, picnic tables, and a small beach at the lower park<br/>- Combine with Wharf Street or other Old Port stops — about a 15-20 min drive from downtown Portland<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/Fort+Williams+Park/@43.6225834,-70.2106029,15z]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
