    <Point>
      <coordinates>-70.2106029,43.6225834,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Fort at No 4 Charlestown NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[The Fort at No. 4<br/><br/>📍 267 Springfield Road, Charlestown, NH<br/><br/>Originally a mid-18th-century stockade protecting Plantation Number 4 — the northernmost British settlement on the Connecticut River in the Province of New Hampshire until after the French and Indian War. Reconstructed in 1960 as an open-air living-history museum focused on 18th-century Upper Valley life, staffed by historians and reenactors depicting the fort's King George's War era. Hosts French and Indian War and Revolutionary War reenactment events most summers.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Seasonal operation — check current hours before visiting.<br/>- A genuinely immersive stop for colonial/frontier history, not just a static museum.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.2548%2C%20-72.4313]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
