  <Placemark>
    <name>Groveton Covered Bridge Northumberland NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Groveton Covered Bridge<br/><br/>📍 Groveton village, Northumberland, NH<br/><br/>Built in 1852 by Captain Charles P. Richardson and his son from native spruce cut on nearby Cape Horn Mountain and milled locally, this 126-foot Paddleford-truss bridge (with arches) is the last of five covered bridges that once stood in Northumberland. It carried vehicle traffic over the Upper Ammonoosuc River until a new Route 3 bridge opened in 1939; restored in 1964–65 by Milton and Arnold Graton, and again in 2020. Open to foot traffic only today.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A quick, easy walk-through stop in the middle of Groveton village.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.5956%2C%20-71.5105]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
