  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Guildhall Village Historic District VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Guildhall Village Historic District<br/><br/>📍 Guildhall, VT (across the Connecticut River from Northumberland, NH)<br/><br/>First settled in the 1780s when a dam and sawmill went up on the Connecticut River, Guildhall became the shire town of Essex County and the first village established in Vermont's Northeast Kingdom. The historic district centers on a square common formed by a bend in VT Route 102, ringed by the 1851 Greek Revival county courthouse, the 1844 Congregational church, and a former jail complex from around 1880. Along the riverbank nearby are the remains of the 1787 Ward Bailey Gristmill and a 1921 hydroelectric plant destroyed by fire in 1963. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1980.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A quiet, essentially untouristed Vermont village green — a nice contrast stop if coming from the busier NH side of the river.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.5625%2C%20-71.5618]]></description>
