  <Placemark>
    <name>Harpswell Meetinghouse Harpswell ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Harpswell Meetinghouse<br/><br/>📍 Route 123, Harpswell Center, Harpswell, ME<br/><br/>Built starting in the late 1750s (not fully completed until the mid-1770s), this is the oldest surviving colonial meetinghouse in Maine — a simple, clapboard-sided, two-story building with a pulpit facing a three-sided gallery, typical of New England meetinghouse design. It served as both church and town hall from 1757 until the parish built a separate church across the road in 1843; the town took ownership in 1856 and it's still used for town meetings today. Designated a National Historic Landmark in 1968.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- The building itself is simple and unadorned — the significance is historical/architectural rather than a showpiece interior.<br/>- For guided tour information, contact the Harpswell Historical Society directly (harpshistory@gmail.com) — it's not a staffed daily-visit site.<br/>- Combine with the rest of Harpswell's historic sites; the Harpswell Historical Society maintains a broader list of the town's colonial-era locations.<br/>- A quiet, low-key stop — good for a short detour rather than a dedicated half-day visit.]]></description>
