  <Placemark>
    <name>Haverhill-Bath Covered Bridge Woodsville NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Haverhill-Bath Covered Bridge<br/><br/>📍 Woodsville, Haverhill, NH (over the Ammonoosuc River)<br/><br/>Built in 1829 with joint funding from the towns of Haverhill and Bath, after original supervisor Airel Miner stepped down and Moses Abbott and Leonard Walker took over construction. Believed to be New Hampshire's oldest covered bridge, and the oldest surviving Ithiel Town lattice truss bridge in the United States. It carried NH Route 135 until 1999, was restored in 2004, and now serves foot traffic only — at 256 feet, it's long but not NH's longest (that's the nearby 400-foot Bath Bridge).<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Pair with the separate Bath covered bridge nearby for a two-bridge stop.<br/>- A short, easy walk-through — good quick add to a drive along the Ammonoosuc valley.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.1547%2C%20-72.0364]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
