  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Holy Resurrection Orthodox Church Berlin NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Holy Resurrection Orthodox Church<br/><br/>📍 20 Petrograd Street, Berlin, NH<br/><br/>Built in just five months in 1915, led by Reverend Arcady Piotrowsky, to serve the roughly 500 Russian immigrants then living in Berlin — mostly single men from the Minsk, Volyn, and Grodno provinces of what's now Belarus and Ukraine, drawn by the city's booming lumber industry. Mill strikes in the 1920s and the Great Depression in the 1930s drove many parishioners away in search of work elsewhere; the church closed in 1963 and sat dormant until reopening in 1974 for the funeral of a longtime Russian immigrant parishioner. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A distinctive onion-domed landmark that speaks to a little-known chapter of New Hampshire's immigrant labor history.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.4677%2C%20-71.1917]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Historical</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>scroll</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>green</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-71.1917,44.4677,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Holy Resurrection Orthodox Church Claremont NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Holy Resurrection Orthodox Church<br/><br/>📍 Claremont, NH<br/><br/>Founded in 1909 when the Brotherhood of the Holy Resurrection formed among Eastern European immigrants — mostly from the area between Moscow and Warsaw near the Polish border — who'd come to work Claremont's booming water-powered mills (paper, cloth, drills, mine and mill machinery). By 1914 the Brotherhood had 55 members and purchased a two-story house, converting the lower floor into a temporary church; an independent parish soon grew to 120 members. The current church building was completed in 1941. Listed on the New Hampshire State Register of Historic Places.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Not to be confused with the identically-named Holy Resurrection Orthodox Church in Berlin, NH — a different parish, founded the same era by a different immigrant community (Russian mill workers rather than the mixed Eastern European community here), in a different part of the state.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.3735%2C%20-72.3456]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
