  <Placemark>
    <name>Joseph and Daniel Marsh House Quechee VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Joseph and Daniel Marsh House<br/><br/>📍 1119 Quechee Main Street, Quechee, VT<br/><br/>Built in 1793 for Joseph Marsh, one of Hartford's early settlers, who led a regiment of colonial militia in the Revolutionary War, helped draft the 1777 constitution of the independent Vermont Republic, and served as Vermont's first Lieutenant Governor under Thomas Chittenden. His son Charles, a noted lawyer, was raised here and later fathered conservationist George Perkins Marsh. The complex — main house, three barns, and a wagon shed/hen house — was moved a short distance in the 1960s due to an Army Corps of Engineers flood-control project. Now operates as the Quechee Inn. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1998.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A working inn — stay overnight, or dine there, to experience the property beyond a drive-by.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.6485%2C%20-72.4075]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
