  <Placemark>
    <name>Libby Island Lighthouse Machiasport ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Libby Island Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Libby Island, Machiasport, ME (entrance to Machias Bay)<br/><br/>The original stone tower, built in the summer and fall of 1822, collapsed within months from poor workmanship and frozen mortar; Noah Humphrey rebuilt it soon after. Libby Island earned its reputation as America's foggiest lighthouse station, averaging 1,554 fog hours annually, which required a large keeper staff and heavy fog-signal infrastructure. Automated and transferred to the Maine Coastal Islands National Wildlife Refuge in 1998.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Grounds and tower closed to the public — best seen by boat through Bold Coast Charters or Bar Harbor Whale Watch.<br/>- Far Down East; a full-day undertaking from Bar Harbor.]]></description>
