  <Placemark>
    <name>Little River State Park Campground Waterbury VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Little River State Park Campground<br/><br/>📍 Waterbury Reservoir, Waterbury, VT<br/><br/>Built in the wake of Vermont's catastrophic November 1927 flood, which killed 55 people and caused over $22 million in damage statewide. Between 1935 and 1938, the Civilian Conservation Corps built Waterbury Dam and Reservoir alongside the Army Corps of Engineers to control future flooding — and housed the workforce in CCC Camp Smith, a fully self-contained community of over 80 buildings holding up to 2,000 men, complete with a hospital, theater, school, church, and ice rink. Only scattered chimneys and concrete foundations remain today, some marked along a self-guided history trail through the park.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- One of central Vermont's most popular parks — reserve well ahead for camping in peak season.<br/>- Combine with a paddle or swim on Waterbury Reservoir itself.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.3898%2C%20-72.7673]]></description>
