  <Placemark>
    <name>Lutheran Stone Church Red Hook NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Lutheran Stone Church<br/><br/>📍 Route 9, Red Hook, NY<br/><br/>This 1786 structure replaces a log church ca. 1730 and housed a congregation dating back to the Palatine immigration of 1715.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Stop 28 of 33 on the Rhinebeck, Red Hook & Milan Scenic Tour (Tour 1).<br/><br/>Quick Read<br/>- Built: 1786 — stone replacement for a ca. 1730 log structure.<br/>- Congregation: Continuous since the Palatine immigration of 1715 — one of the oldest Lutheran flocks in the Hudson Valley.<br/>- Style: Vernacular stone, austere, fortress-squat — exactly what you'd expect from cash-strapped German immigrants who'd outlasted persecution and wilderness.<br/>- Stop 28/33 on the Rhinebeck, Red Hook & Milan Scenic Tour (Tour 1).<br/><br/>Vibe<br/>Plain, pious, and proud. No spires begging the sky — just thick walls holding three centuries of Palatine memory. Worth a pull-off; photographers will like the texture, historians will like the timeline.<br/><br/>One-Liner<br/>"1786 stone; 1730 log beneath it; 1715 souls beneath that — Route 9's quiet Palatine witness."]]></description>
