  <Placemark>
    <name>Main Street Museum White River Junction VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Main Street Museum<br/><br/>📍 White River Junction, VT (former fire station, Bridge Street)<br/><br/>Founded in 1992 by career artist David Fairbanks Ford in "The Old South End," inspired by 18th-century cabinets of curiosities. The collection is deliberately unfocused: taxidermy and biological anomalies (dehydrated cats among them), miniature shoes, a huge collected-key display, sheet music, journals, electromagnetic devices, Elvis impersonator memorabilia — and genuinely odd specifics like Elvis Presley's gallstones, presidential hair clippings, and dirt from outside Charles Lindbergh's house. Housed in the town's former fire station, next to the railroad underpass on the White River.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open by appointment or during events only since the pandemic — check ahead rather than dropping by unannounced.<br/>- A genuinely odd, memorable stop if you can time it right — not a conventional museum experience.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.6500%2C%20-72.3190]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
