  <Placemark>
    <name>Maine Maritime Museum Bath ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Maine Maritime Museum<br/><br/>📍 243 Washington St, Bath, ME<br/><br/>A 20-acre waterfront campus centered on the Percy & Small Shipyard, designated a National Historic Landmark in 1994 as the only surviving U.S. shipyard that built large wooden sailing vessels — 42 giant wooden schooners were launched here between 1894 and 1920, including the Wyoming (1909), the largest wooden sailing ship ever built in America. The broader museum covers Maine's shipbuilding and maritime history well beyond just the shipyard itself.<br/><br/>Must See<br/>- Percy & Small Shipyard — guided tours daily at 11am and 1pm (mid-May through October), included with admission.<br/>- The Bath waterfront setting itself, on the Kennebec River where shipbuilding is still an active industry today (Bath Iron Works is nearby).<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open daily, 9:30am–5pm, year-round.<br/>- Admission is good for two days if you want to split the visit; kids 17 and under are free.<br/>- Combine with a look at the active Bath Iron Works shipyard nearby — Bath's shipbuilding history is very much a living industry, not just a museum piece.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.mainemaritimemuseum.org/]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
