  <Placemark>
    <name>Major Jacob Hasbrouck Jr House New Paltz NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Major Jacob Hasbrouck Jr. House<br/><br/>📍 193 Huguenot St, New Paltz, NY<br/><br/>Built in 1786 by Jacob Hasbrouck Jr., grandson of Jean Hasbrouck, one of the original Huguenot settlers of New Paltz — a descendant lived here continuously into recent times, making it believed to be the only 18th-century stone house in the New Paltz area never to have left its founding family. Represents the refined culmination of the Dutch/Belgian stone-house style built across Ulster County over the preceding century. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1999.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Sits along Historic Huguenot Street — walkable as part of a Huguenot Street visit rather than a separate stop.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=41.7606%2C%20-74.0864]]></description>
