  <Placemark>
    <name>Matinicus Rock Lighthouse Knox County ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Matinicus Rock Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Matinicus Rock, ~27 miles from Rockland, ME<br/><br/>Built in 1827 with twin wooden towers, rebuilt in granite in 1846, and again in 1857. Famous for 17-year-old Abbie Burgess, who single-handedly kept the lights burning for a month during a devastating 1856 gale while her father was ashore: "Under God I was able to perform all my accustomed duties as well as my father's." Waves have been known to wash over structures 48 feet above high water — one of the most exposed light stations on the Atlantic Coast.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Closed to the public; owned by U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service as part of the Maine Coastal Islands National Wildlife Refuge.<br/>- Boat or air access only — Matinicus Excursions runs occasional trips.<br/>- A Fresnel lens from the station is on display at the Maine Lighthouse Museum in Rockland.]]></description>
