  <Placemark>
    <name>Nyquist-Harcourt Wildlife Sanctuary New Paltz NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Nyquist-Harcourt Wildlife Sanctuary<br/><br/>📍 Near Historic Huguenot Street, New Paltz, NY<br/><br/>A 56-acre sanctuary along the Wallkill River, centered on half an oxbow — a complex of ponds and wetlands left behind when the river straightened its course. 153 bird species have been recorded over a 12-year period, 36 of them on New York's Audubon conservation-concern list. Renamed in 2010 when the Nyquist family took over ownership; now managed by the Wallkill Valley Land Trust. A 2-mile loop trail covers the property.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open dawn to dusk daily, year-round; leashed pets only, no bikes or horses.<br/>- No bathrooms or picnic tables — pack in, pack out.<br/>- Easy walk from Historic Huguenot Street if combining both stops.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=41.7544%2C%20-74.0864]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
