    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Oak Point Farm Boothbay ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Oak Point Farm<br/><br/>📍 60 Samoset Road, Boothbay Harbor, ME<br/><br/>A 32-acre property with over 2,000 feet of coastline on Hodgdon Cove, described as the last remaining undeveloped saltwater farm in Boothbay Harbor — now the headquarters of the Boothbay Region Land Trust. It's the most amenity-rich of the BRLT preserves: a 0.6-mile accessible shoreline route suitable for wheelchairs and strollers, an easy 1-mile loop through fields and forest, a 2-acre freshwater pond for wildlife viewing, a deep-water dock with kayak launch, and three courtesy moorings. In summer the milkweed fields draw hundreds of monarch butterflies.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open dawn to dusk, seven days a week; the visitor center/farmhouse itself is staffed weekdays 9:30am–4:30pm.<br/>- Preserve parking is on the left, with disability parking beside the farmhouse.<br/>- Public restrooms and a small Nature Center exhibit are on site — one of the few preserves with real facilities.<br/>- Good pick if the group includes anyone who needs the fully accessible route rather than a woods trail.<br/>- Winter brings cross-country skiing and snowshoeing on the maintained trails.<br/>- The mowed field is open for picnicking or a quick outdoor stretch/yoga break.]]></description>
