  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Ocean Point Preserve Boothbay ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Ocean Point Preserve<br/><br/>📍 Van Horn Road, East Boothbay, ME<br/><br/>A 15-acre Boothbay Region Land Trust preserve within Linekin Neck's largest wetland, centered on Tibbetts Pond — designated by Maine's Department of Inland Fisheries and Wildlife as significant waterfowl habitat. Two easy trails: the white-blazed Norman Van Horn Trail (~0.9 mile loop) and the shorter blue-blazed Golden Crowned Kinglet Trail (~0.2 mile) to a wetland observation point. It sits right along the scenic Route 96 drive out to Ocean Point itself — a narrow coastal road winding past houses perched on the rocky shore, ending at a point established as a summer retreat back in 1876, overlooking several islands and lighthouses.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- About a 7-mile, ~15-minute scenic drive southeast from downtown Boothbay Harbor via Route 96 — worth doing as a loop with the drive itself, not just the trail.<br/>- Small parking area on Van Horn Road, 0.3 miles off Route 96 — roadside parking isn't permitted, so use the lot.<br/>- Good birding in warmer months; the pond is used for ice skating in winter.<br/>- Combine with continuing to the end of Route 96 at Ocean Point for open water views and a look at nearby lighthouses.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
