  <Placemark>
    <name>Otter Cliff &amp; Otter Point Acadia National Park ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Otter Cliff & Otter Point<br/><br/>📍 Acadia National Park ME<br/><br/>A dramatic 110-foot granite headland — one of the highest Atlantic coastal cliffs north of Rio de Janeiro — reached via an easy, flat stretch of the Ocean Path just south of Thunder Hole. Otter Point, immediately adjacent, is a broad rock ledge popular for tidepooling and sunrise. Both are simple walks along a clifftop path, not a climb or scramble.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Reached via the flat Ocean Path — you view the cliffs from a walking trail, no exposed scrambling involved.<br/>- A popular sunrise spot with far fewer crowds than Cadillac Mountain.<br/>- Otter Point's tidal ledges are good for tidepooling at low tide.<br/>- Small parking area 0.7 miles past Thunder Hole on Park Loop Road; fills quickly in season.]]></description>
