  <Placemark>
    <name>Ovens Mouth Preserve Boothbay ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Ovens Mouth Preserve<br/><br/>📍 Dover Road, Boothbay, ME (East and West sections)<br/><br/>The largest Boothbay Region Land Trust preserve — two peninsulas connected by a 93-foot pedestrian bridge, with over 5 miles of combined trail. The East section (60 acres) has an easier 1.1-mile loop plus a shorter 0.5-mile option, good for beginners; the West section (86 acres) is more demanding, with 3.6 miles of trail including a 1.75-mile loop. Both sides overlook tidal basins and salt marsh, with striking blue-green water color at tide changes — best viewed at low tide, when remnants of a historical dam are also visible.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- East parking: from the Boothbay Center roundabout, Route 27 north 1.6 mi, left on Adams Pond Road (0.1 mi), right on Dover Road (1.9 mi), bear right onto Dover Extension — lot at the end.<br/>- West parking: same route to Dover Road but only 1.9 mi, then bear left onto Dover Cross Road — lot 0.2 mi on the right.<br/>- Time your visit around low tide for the dramatic tidal color and to see the old dam remnants.<br/>- Dogs must be leashed at all times.<br/>- Pick East if you want an easier walk, West if you want real mileage and more varied terrain.<br/>- Multiple trailheads mean you can spread out and avoid crowding even in peak season — a real local favorite for that reason.]]></description>
