  <Placemark>
    <name>Parking - Downtown Damariscotta ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Time: 2026-08-10T11:00:00<br/><br/>Parking — Downtown Damariscotta<br/><br/>📍 Cross St / Water St, Damariscotta, ME (First National Bank lot)<br/><br/>The largest public lot near Main Street — about 60 spaces, a short walk to Main Street shops and restaurants. A smaller public lot also exists at Bangor Savings Bank (100 Main St, ~15 spaces) if this one is full.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Best central option for a Main Street walk — everything (Skidompha Bookshop, Ann's Book Bistro, Barn Door Baking Co, King Eider's Pub) is within a few blocks.<br/>- Damariscotta is far less crowded than Pemaquid Point — parking pressure here is mild except during the summer farmers market.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-gray</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Parking</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>parking</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>gray</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-69.530317,44.0318737,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Parking - Downtown Wiscasset ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Time: 2026-08-10T15:00:00<br/><br/>Parking — Downtown Wiscasset<br/><br/>📍 Railroad Ave, Wiscasset, ME — recommended for 8/10 (Water St near Red's Eats is the backup)<br/><br/>Wiscasset is a classic Route 1 bottleneck in summer — Main Street itself is the highway, and the line for Red's Eats can back up traffic through town. Parking is free and more plentiful than the traffic makes it look; the town has multiple municipal lots on both sides of Main St, with signage pointing to each.<br/><br/>- ⭐ Railroad Avenue — the pick for tomorrow. A newer, paved, lit municipal lot right on the water, with a short walk back along the waterfront into the village. Coming from the north (Bath/Camden direction), it's the first lot you reach.<br/>- Water Street (backup) — a lot plus plentiful on-street parking, steps from Red's Eats and the Main St shops. Best if Railroad Ave is full or you're approaching from the south (Portland/Brunswick direction).<br/>- Additional municipal lots exist on Middle Street and at the Municipal Building if both of the above are full.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Don't try to street-park right on Main St/Route 1 during midday in summer — that's the traffic bottleneck itself, not a parking strategy. Use one of the side-street lots instead.<br/>- If Red's Eats is the main draw, the Water St lot is the shortest walk.<br/>- Overnight on-street parking is banned town-wide Nov 15–Apr 15 — not relevant for an Aug/Sep visit, but worth knowing if plans shift into shoulder season.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-gray</styleUrl>
