  <Placemark>
    <name>Pemaquid Point Lighthouse ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Time: 2026-08-07T08:30:00<br/><br/>Pemaquid Point Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 3115 Bristol Rd, New Harbor, ME<br/><br/>Commissioned in 1827 by John Quincy Adams, this is one of Maine's most photographed lighthouses — its image appears on Maine's state quarter. Set on dramatic open-ocean granite ledges, it draws roughly 100,000 visitors a year. The keeper's house holds the Fishermen's Museum, covering the region's working maritime history.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Rating is well-supported here — Tripadvisor alone shows 4.8 over 903 reviews, a large enough sample to trust.<br/>- $4/person park admission; $1 suggested donation to climb the tower. Open seasonally, mid-May through late October.<br/>- The granite ledges are dramatic but genuinely slippery — mind footing, especially with kids.<br/>- Pair with Colonial Pemaquid State Historic Site and Fort William Henry nearby.]]></description>
