      <Data name="color"><value>green</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-70.2499928,43.657787,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Portland Head Light Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Portland Head Light<br/><br/>📍 1000 Shore Road, Cape Elizabeth, ME (Fort Williams Park)<br/><br/>Portland Head Light is Maine's oldest and most photographed lighthouse, commissioned in 1791 by President George Washington and first lit in 1791. It sits on a dramatic rocky headland inside Fort Williams Park in Cape Elizabeth, about 4 miles from downtown Portland.<br/><br/>The grounds are free to walk and open year-round. The adjacent Museum at Portland Head Light (seasonal) is housed in the former keeper's quarters.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- The lighthouse exterior and park grounds are always free; the museum charges a small admission fee<br/>- Best light for photography is early morning or late afternoon<br/>- The surrounding park has great ocean views, picnic areas, and ruins of old coastal fortifications<br/>- Plan 45–60 minutes for the visit; combine with a drive along the Cape Elizabeth coast<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/Portland+Head+Light/@43.6234,-70.2072,17z]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Lighthouse</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>anchor</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>purple</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-70.2072,43.6234,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Portland Masonic Temple Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Portland Masonic Temple<br/><br/>📍 415 Congress Street, Portland, ME<br/><br/>The Portland Masonic Temple at 415 Congress Street is an example of Beaux Arts Classicism commercial architecture, erected in 1911. It is home to several active Masonic organizations dedicated to preserving and restoring its incredible interior spaces.<br/><br/>One of the most remarkable spaces is the two-story Corinthian Hall, featuring Corinthian columns on all four walls reaching to the ceiling. The hall also houses one of Maine's largest working pipe organs — second only to the Kotzschmar Organ next door at Merrill Auditorium.<br/><br/>History buff tip: Check out the Maine Masonic Civil War Library & Museum on the third floor.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/415+Congress+St,+Portland,+ME]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Attraction</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>star</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>purple</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-70.257817,43.6587121,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Portland Museum of Art Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Portland Museum of Art<br/><br/>📍 7 Congress Square, Portland, ME<br/><br/>The Portland Museum of Art (PMA) is Maine's largest and most celebrated art museum, founded in 1882. Its primary building was designed by I.M. Pei & Partners (1983) and is itself an architectural landmark.<br/><br/>The collection shines brightest with Maine and American artists: Winslow Homer (the museum holds the largest collection of his works), Andrew Wyeth, Edward Hopper, Rockwell Kent, and John Singer Sargent are all well represented.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Free admission on Friday evenings 4–8 PM<br/>- The Winslow Homer collection alone is worth the visit — he spent his later years on the Maine coast<br/>- Steps from the Wadsworth-Longfellow House; easy to combine into a single Congress Street afternoon<br/>- Check for special exhibitions; the PMA hosts major traveling shows throughout the year<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/Portland+Museum+of+Art/@43.6567,-70.2573,17z]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Museum</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>landmark</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>green</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-70.2573,43.6567,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Portland Observatory Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Portland Observatory<br/><br/>📍 Portland ME<br/><br/>The only surviving 19th-century maritime signal tower in the United States, built in 1807 on Munjoy Hill. Captains paid an annual fee to have their ships' arrivals flagged from the octagonal summit, giving them time to arrange stevedores before docking. The 360° view of Casco Bay and the city is the best in Portland.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- The guided climb is the only way to access the summit — tours run every 30 minutes.<br/>- The view looking east over Casco Bay at dusk is particularly fine.<br/>- Combine with a walk along the Eastern Promenade immediately below.]]></description>
