  <Placemark>
    <name>Rock of Ages Granite Quarry Barre VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Rock of Ages Granite Quarry<br/><br/>📍 Barre VT<br/><br/>The largest granite quarrying operation in the world — an open-pit mine that has been cutting since 1885, now dropping more than 600 feet into the earth and spanning several city blocks. A free public viewing gallery looks over the edge of a pit where workers and equipment are rendered ant-sized by the scale. The quarry produces the gray granite found in monuments, memorials, and government buildings across the country, including the Tomb of the Unknowns.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- The free outdoor Craftsman Center and viewing gallery are open Mon–Sat, 8:30am–5pm.<br/>- Paid factory tours (the finishing shed where granite is cut and polished) run in summer — worth booking.<br/>- The nearby Hope Cemetery is an outdoor gallery of elaborate granite monuments carved by the quarry workers' descendants.<br/>- 35 minutes from Stowe via Route 2 — a natural addition to a Burlington or Montpelier day.]]></description>
