  <Placemark>
    <name>Russell-Colbath House Albany NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Russell-Colbath House<br/><br/>📍 Passaconaway, Albany, NH (Kancamagus Highway)<br/><br/>Built around 1831 by Thomas Russell and his son Amzi, this is the only surviving early homestead in the Swift River valley. The property passed down through the family to Ruth Priscilla Colbath and her husband Thomas Alden Colbath; when the house became Passaconaway's post office in the 1890s, Ruth was its first postmistress. On a fall day in 1891, Thomas told Ruth he'd "be back in a little while" — and was never seen again. She reportedly kept a light burning in the window for him until her own death. The U.S. Forest Service bought the property in 1961 to preserve it; listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1987.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Free to visit; staffed seasonally with rangers who tell the full story.<br/>- A quick, easy stop right off the highway — pairs well with a Rocky Gorge or Sabbaday Falls visit nearby.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.9957%2C%20-71.3398]]></description>
