  <Placemark>
    <name>Saco River Covered Bridge Conway NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Saco River Covered Bridge<br/><br/>📍 East Side Road, Conway, NH<br/><br/>Also known as the Conway Covered Bridge — a 225-foot Paddleford truss (with added arches) built in 1890, restored 1987-89, with a posted six-ton limit. History has a strange echo here: during an 1869 flood, the Swift River Covered Bridge (in Albany, near the eastern end of the Kancamagus) was swept downstream and crashed into this bridge, carrying both two miles downriver together.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Recently closed to vehicles after a crash — repairs were expected to take months as of the most recent reporting; still open to pedestrians on the walkways. Verify current status before planning to drive across.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.9989%2C%20-71.1089]]></description>
