  <Placemark>
    <name>Saddleback Ledge Lighthouse Isle au Haut ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Saddleback Ledge Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Saddleback Ledge, between Vinalhaven and Isle au Haut, ME<br/><br/>Built 1838–40 on a barren granite ledge at the entrance to Isle au Haut Bay — a 40.6-foot conical tower designed by architect Alexander Parris and built by Gridley J.F. Bryant. Early keeper Watson Hopkins reported miserably isolated conditions despite the district's highest salary ($450/year). In a famous 1927 storm, 124 ducks collided with the lantern windows; a 10-pound drake cracked the lens and knocked out the light.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Owned and operated by the U.S. Coast Guard; grounds and tower closed to the public.<br/>- Viewable only by boat or plane — Isle au Haut Boat Services runs excursions past it.]]></description>
