  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Schoodic Peninsula Day 1 Park Loop Trails Points</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Schoodic Peninsula: Day 1 — Park Loop, Trails & Points<br/><br/>A full day exploring the core Schoodic Peninsula experience: the scenic loop road, moderate hikes with big views, and coastal access, all while dodging peak crowds.<br/><br/>Itinerary<br/><br/>Morning: Arrive via Ferry (8:30–9:00 a.m.)<br/>Take the Downeast Windjammer Ferry from Bar Harbor (45-minute scenic crossing that avoids Ellsworth road congestion). Arrive at Winter Harbor by 9:00 a.m.<br/><br/>Early Morning: Frazer Point (9:00–9:45 a.m.)<br/>Start at Frazer Point Picnic Area to enjoy the docks and rocky shoreline before crowds arrive. Coffee, photos, tide pool exploration.<br/><br/>Mid-Morning: Schoodic Loop Road (9:45 a.m.–12:30 p.m.)<br/>Bike or drive the one-way Schoodic Loop Road, stopping at pull-offs for lighthouse and island views. Skip the main lots; use pullouts instead.<br/><br/>Key stops:<br/>- Schoodic Point scenic viewpoints<br/>- Lighthouse and coastal overlooks<br/>- Island views across Frenchman Bay<br/><br/>Late Morning into Early Afternoon: Anvil Trail & Raven's Nest Hike (12:30–2:30 p.m.)<br/>Hike Anvil Trail to Schoodic Head (2 miles out-and-back, moderate climb to 440 feet). Sweeping 360-degree views of Frenchman Bay, Cadillac Mountain, Bar Harbor, and two lighthouses. Detour to Raven's Nest cliff-top viewpoint on return for sunset photography spot.<br/><br/>Lunch (2:30–3:15 p.m.)<br/>The Pickled Wrinkle in Birch Harbor — local favorite seafood, well below Bar Harbor wait times and prices.<br/><br/>Late Afternoon: Schoodic Point & Tide Pooling (3:15–5:00 p.m.)<br/>Return to Schoodic Point itself for late-afternoon tide pooling (low tide window) or wave-watching at high tide. Avoid midday when lots fill; this window is optimal for solitude and wildlife.<br/><br/>Evening: Return to Bar Harbor<br/>Evening ferry back to Bar Harbor (or drive via the scenic byway if time permits).<br/><br/>Notes<br/>- Timing: The early start and ferry arrival avoid peak midday congestion<br/>- Parking: Use pullouts rather than main lots on the Schoodic Loop Road<br/>- Crowds: Schoodic receives only ~8% of Acadia's total visitors; expect far fewer people than Mount Desert Island trails<br/>- Weather: Pack layers; the peninsula is windier and cooler than Bar Harbor<br/>- Food: The Pickled Wrinkle is genuinely quieter than downtown; no need to rush back to Bar Harbor for dinner]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-orange</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>day-trip</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>gem</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>orange</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-68.0610631,44.3323041,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Schoodic Peninsula Day 2 Villages Byway Water</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Schoodic Peninsula: Day 2 — Villages, Byway & Water<br/><br/>A full day exploring the lesser-known side of Schoodic: the 29-mile National Scenic Byway beyond the park, genuine working fishing villages, gravel trail networks, and tide-dependent water explorations.<br/><br/>Itinerary<br/><br/>Morning: Winter Harbor & Coffee (8:30–9:15 a.m.)<br/>Start in Winter Harbor, ME (assuming you stayed overnight or arrived early via ferry). Grab coffee and explore the 5&10 general store—quintessential Maine village experience.<br/><br/>Late Morning: Schoodic National Scenic Byway Drive (9:15 a.m.–11:00 a.m.)<br/>Drive the Schoodic National Scenic Byway (extends 29 miles beyond the park loop and is almost entirely ignored by tourists who only do the 6-mile park section). <br/><br/>Key stops:<br/>- Corea — genuine working lobster village with zero tourist infrastructure, authentic Maine fishing community<br/>- U.S. Bell on the way — working bell foundry, casual visits welcome, genuinely unique artisan stop<br/><br/>Mid-Morning into Midday: Schoodic Bike Path Network (11:00 a.m.–1:30 p.m.)<br/>Bike or walk the Schoodic Bike Path Network (8.3-mile gravel trail system connecting Frazer Point, Wonsqueak Harbor, Bunker Harbor, Birch Harbor Mountain). Sees a fraction of the traffic of the auto loop. Start from Schoodic Woods Campground ranger station lot.<br/><br/>Trail segments:<br/>- Frazer Point to Wonsqueak Harbor<br/>- Bunker Harbor connection<br/>- Birch Harbor Mountain views<br/><br/>Lunch (1:30–2:15 p.m.)<br/>The Pickled Wrinkle in Birch Harbor (or bring a picnic if you prefer eating on the trail).<br/><br/>Early Afternoon: Sundew Trail Nature Walk (2:15–3:00 p.m.)<br/>Walk the flat, easy Sundew Trail from the Schoodic Institute to private rocky shoreline (0.7 miles). Often completely empty. Excellent for birding and quiet nature observation.<br/><br/>Afternoon: Tide-Dependent Little Moose Island Exploration (timing depends on tides)<br/>CRITICAL: Time this around low tide. Check tide charts before starting.<br/><br/>Walk from Blueberry Hill out toward Little Moose Island at low tide (unique tidal excursion most visitors never attempt). Requires sturdy boots, tide awareness, and good timing—but rewards with genuine exploration feeling and unique coastal geology.<br/><br/>Late Afternoon: Optional Ranger Program (if available)<br/>If the Schoodic Institute is running a free birding walk or other ranger program that day, join it for insider-style activity with limited capacity and almost no crowd overlap with Day 1.<br/><br/>Evening: Dinner in Winter Harbor or Birch Harbor<br/>Wrap up dinner back in Winter Harbor or Birch Harbor rather than backtracking toward Bar Harbor. Local restaurants stay quieter and less touristy than downtown.<br/><br/>Notes<br/>- Timing: The Schoodic Byway is genuinely ignored; expect almost no crowds outside the park loop<br/>- Biking: Hybrid or mountain bike recommended for gravel trails; walking is also fine<br/>- Tide Planning: Little Moose Island walk is only possible within ~2-3 hours of low tide. Check tide charts and plan your afternoon accordingly.<br/>- Weather: Bring layers; the byway is windier than Bar Harbor<br/>- Food: Staying in the Schoodic area for dinner means you skip the evening rush back to downtown Bar Harbor<br/>- Unique Experiences: This itinerary includes things almost no other tourists do on a Bar Harbor visit (Corea, the bell foundry, the bike paths, the tide walk)]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-orange</styleUrl>
