  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Shepard Hill Historic District Holderness NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Shepard Hill Historic District<br/><br/>📍 Shepard Hill Road, Holderness, NH<br/><br/>One of the earliest summer-estate enclaves near Squam Lake, this district holds 17 historic houses built between 1870 and 1921, plus a chapel, on a stretch of Shepard Hill Road with expansive lake views. The first house, "The Pines," was built in 1870 by a Connecticut couple; a hotel built near the summit in 1881 hosted poet John Greenleaf Whittier among its guests before being demolished in 1948. Listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2014.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A quiet drive past private summer homes — the Chapel of the Holy Cross is the one publicly visible landmark within the district.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.7256%2C%20-71.5706]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
