  <Placemark>
    <name>Smugglers Notch Stowe VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Smugglers Notch<br/><br/>📍 Stowe VT<br/><br/>Route 108 north of Stowe squeezes through a boulder-strewn mountain pass so narrow that large vehicles cannot make it through. During the Revolution and Prohibition, the notch served as a smuggling corridor to Canada — too tight and remote for authorities. The road winds between boulders the size of houses that fell from the cliffs above and were never cleared. The 10-mile drive to Jeffersonville and back is one of the most dramatic short excursions in Vermont.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Route 108 through the Notch is closed in winter — accessible only from late May through October.<br/>- The road is too narrow for RVs, trailers, or buses — car only.<br/>- Several pullouts allow you to stop and walk among the enormous fallen boulders.<br/>- The road continues to Smugglers Notch Resort and the village of Jeffersonville — a pleasant destination for lunch.<br/>- The pass peaks at 2,162 feet — noticeably cooler than Stowe village.]]></description>
