  <Placemark>
    <name>Spring Point Ledge Lighthouse South Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Spring Point Ledge Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 End of the breakwater near Fort Road, South Portland, ME<br/><br/>Built after decades of shipwrecks on the ledge, including the dramatic 1876 wreck of the bark Harriet S. Jackson, and after steamship companies carrying 500,000+ passengers a year petitioned for a light. Activated May 24, 1897; automated in 1960. One of only three surviving "spark plug" caisson lights in Maine.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open to the public in summer via the Spring Point Ledge Lighthouse Trust — tower access is occasional, but it was the first caisson lighthouse in the U.S. opened for tours (1999).<br/>- Visitor center and gift shop added in 2017; an easy walk out the breakwater from Fort Williams-adjacent parking.]]></description>
