  <Placemark>
    <name>Stanwood Homestead Ellsworth ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Stanwood Homestead (Birdsacre)<br/><br/>📍 289 High St (Route 3), Ellsworth, ME<br/><br/>The childhood home of Cordelia Stanwood (1865–1958), a self-taught ornithologist and one of the first American women to publish scientific nature photography and bird-behavior research. She donated the land to preserve it, and it's now Birdsacre — a 200-acre sanctuary with walking trails, gardens, a small nature museum in the homestead, and an on-site raptor rehabilitation center housing non-releasable owls and hawks.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- A good stop for kids given the resident owls and hawks at the rehab enclosures.<br/>- Trails are easy and mostly flat — a low-key break on the drive between Ellsworth and Bar Harbor.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.3142%2C%20-68.2418]]></description>
