  <Placemark>
    <name>Storm King Art Center New Windsor NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Storm King Art Center<br/><br/>📍 New Windsor NY<br/><br/>500 acres of rolling Hudson Valley farmland and forest installed with large-scale sculpture by the most significant artists of the 20th and 21st centuries: Alexander Calder's monumental stabiles, Mark di Suvero's soaring steel constructions, Isamu Noguchi's granite works, Maya Lin's earthwork Wave Field, and Richard Serra's massive curved Cor-Ten steel walls emerging from hillsides. You discover the works while walking through a landscape — a fundamentally different experience from any indoor museum.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open Apr–Nov; closed Dec–Mar.<br/>- The tram covers the full 500 acres — take it first to orient, then walk back to what interested you.<br/>- The Serra pieces on the hillside above the main meadow are among his finest outdoor works.<br/>- Allow 3–4 hours minimum; many people spend a full day.<br/>- Picnicking is permitted and encouraged — bring lunch and eat among the Calders.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-purple</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Museum</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>landmark</value></Data>
      <Data name="color"><value>purple</value></Data>
    </ExtendedData>
    <Point>
      <coordinates>-74.0192,41.4142,0</coordinates>
    </Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Story Land Glen NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Story Land<br/><br/>📍 850 NH Route 16, Glen, NH<br/><br/>Opened in 1954 by Bob and Ruth Morrell, who'd bought a collection of German storybook-character dolls and hired local art teacher Arlene "Topsy" Samuelson to design a theme park around them. Originally called Story Town (with a single ride — "Freddie the Fire Engine"), renamed Story Land after its first season to avoid confusion with Storytown USA in upstate New York. Now a 35-acre park with 50+ rides, shows, and character meet-and-greets, aimed at kids up through the pre-teen years.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Geared squarely at younger children — teens may find it tame.<br/>- Combine with a Cranmore Mountain or Echo Lake stop nearby in North Conway.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.0654%2C%20-71.2062]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
