  <Placemark>
    <name>Texas Falls Hancock VT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Texas Falls Recreation Area<br/><br/>📍 Route 125, Hancock, VT (Green Mountain National Forest)<br/><br/>An easily accessible roadside waterfall in a narrow rocky gorge, with a bridge crossing overhead for a direct view down into the falls. The gorge began forming roughly 12,000 years ago as Ice Age meltwater cut through glacial till and bedrock, with swirling rocks and debris carving the smooth potholes still visible today — part of the Green Mountain Anticlinorium, a structural arch formed some 460 million years ago. A 1.2-mile loop trail (about 70 ft of elevation change, roughly 1 hour) circles the falls; a large picnic pavilion sits nearby, with fishing access along Hancock Branch and Texas Brook.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Easy and family-friendly — one of the more accessible waterfall stops on this byway.<br/>- Combine with a Granville Gulf / Moss Glen Falls stop, just a few miles south.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=43.9762%2C%20-72.8402]]></description>
