  <Placemark>
    <name>The Basin Franconia Notch NH</name>
    <description><![CDATA[The Basin<br/><br/>📍 Franconia Notch State Park, NH<br/><br/>A 30-foot-diameter, 15-foot-deep glacial pothole in the Pemigewasset River, formed by meltwater as the last Ice Age ended roughly 25,000 years ago and gradually polished smooth by sand and stones ever since. Henry David Thoreau called it "the most remarkable curiosity of its kind in New England" after visiting in 1839.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Paved, accessible pathway right off the parkway — one of the easiest stops in the whole park.<br/>- Connects to the Basin-Cascades Trail if you want to extend into a longer walk along the brook above it.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=44.1032%2C%20-71.6837]]></description>
