  <Placemark>
    <name>Thunder Hole Acadia National Park ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Thunder Hole<br/><br/>📍 Acadia National Park ME<br/><br/>A natural sea cave on the Ocean Path where incoming waves compress air and water into a narrow granite slot, producing a resonating boom and a geyser of spray. The effect is most dramatic at mid-incoming tide with moderate to heavy swells — the combination produces the loudest and most spectacular show. Easy roadside access on the Park Loop Road.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Check the tide table: mid-incoming tide (roughly 2 hours before high) is optimal.<br/>- Rough-sea days amplify the effect dramatically — post-storm conditions can be spectacular.<br/>- Protective railings are in place but spray can reach the observation area — wear something you don't mind getting wet.<br/>- Part of the Ocean Path, a flat 2-mile walk from Sand Beach — do both together.]]></description>
