  <Placemark>
    <name>Wadsworth Longfellow House Portland ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Wadsworth-Longfellow House<br/><br/>📍 487 Congress Street, Portland, ME<br/><br/>The Wadsworth-Longfellow House at 487 Congress Street is the childhood home of celebrated poet and educator Henry Wadsworth Longfellow. Built in 1785–1786, it was the first dwelling in Portland constructed entirely of brick and holds the distinction of being Maine's first house museum open to the public.<br/><br/>Designated a National Historic Landmark, and operated by the Maine Historical Society, which is headquartered on the same downtown site.<br/><br/>Don't miss the stunning Longfellow Garden located behind the house — a wonderful oasis in the heart of Portland's downtown.<br/><br/>See also: Longfellow House Garden Portland ME for garden details.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open May–Oct, Mon–Sat 10am–5pm and Sun noon–4pm (guided tours on the hour at noon and 1pm, self-guided otherwise via a smartphone app); Nov–Apr Tues–Sat 10am–5pm.<br/>- Admission ~$18 adults, $12 students, free for kids 5 and under; house ticket includes the exhibition gallery.<br/>- The garden is free and open May–Oct during regular house hours — worth a stop even without paying for the house tour.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/place/Wadsworth-Longfellow+House/@43.6568,-70.2601,17z]]></description>
