  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>West Quoddy Head Lighthouse Lubec ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[West Quoddy Head Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 West Quoddy Head State Park, Route 189, Lubec, ME<br/><br/>Established in 1808 after local citizens petitioned Congress in 1806, with the current brick tower completed in 1857. It's the easternmost beacon in the United States and one of only two remaining red-and-white striped lighthouses in the country. West Quoddy also pioneered American fog signaling, starting with a fog bell in 1820 and moving to steam whistles by 1868.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Open Tuesday–Saturday 10am–4pm and Sunday 11am–4pm, Memorial Day through mid-October (closed Mondays) — squarely within the trip window.<br/>- Grounds and seasonal dwelling open, tower closed; volunteer-run by the West Quoddy Head Light Keepers Association.<br/>- Pair with Lubec Channel Light and, across the water, Campobello Island (Canada).]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
