  <Placemark>
    <name>West Street Historic District Bar Harbor ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[West Street Historic District<br/><br/>📍 West St, Bar Harbor, ME (Eden St to Billings Ave)<br/><br/>A row of grand Gilded Age summer "cottages" — 17 historic buildings, Late Victorian mansions and stylish inns — built along Frenchman Bay during Bar Harbor's heyday as a resort for America's wealthiest families in the late 19th and early 20th century. Most are now private homes or inns, viewed from the public street rather than toured individually.<br/><br/>Highlights<br/>- La Rochelle — a 41-room red-brick French-château-style mansion, built for an heir to the Campbell Soup Company fortune, now home to the Bar Harbor Historical Society and open to the public seasonally.<br/>- Bar Harbor Club — a large 1929-30 Tudor Revival building, the district's principal non-residential property, once home to an elite social club.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Most properties are private homes — enjoy the architecture from the public sidewalks and tree-lined streets rather than expecting interior access.<br/>- Free, no ticket required — a good easy evening or morning stroll right off downtown Bar Harbor.<br/>- Check La Rochelle's seasonal open hours if you want an actual interior visit via the Historical Society.<br/>- Combine with a downtown Bar Harbor walk — it's directly adjacent to the main village.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
    <ExtendedData>
      <Data name="vault_type"><value>Historical</value></Data>
      <Data name="icon"><value>scroll</value></Data>
