  <Placemark>
    <name>Whitehead Lighthouse Spruce Head ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Whitehead Lighthouse<br/><br/>📍 Whitehead Island, ~2 miles south of Spruce Head, ME<br/><br/>Maine's third lighthouse, established in 1803 after sea captains petitioned for a light guarding the entrance to Penobscot Bay; the current granite tower dates to just after 1852. Engineer Andrew Morse Jr.'s 1838 "perpetual fog bell," powered by tidal motion, was ingenious but ultimately unreliable. Keeper Isaac Grant and his wife Abbie Burgess Grant — famous for singlehandedly keeping Matinicus Rock Light burning during an 1856 gale — served here from 1875–1890 and rescued two drowning men in 1881.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Closed to general visitors, but available for week-long rentals through Pine Island Camp, which owns the property.<br/>- Otherwise best seen by boat via Monhegan Boat Line cruises from Port Clyde.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
