  <Placemark>
    <name>Widow Jane Mine Rosendale NY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Widow Jane Mine<br/><br/>📍 Snyder Estate, Rosendale, NY<br/><br/>Established in 1825 after engineers building the Delaware & Hudson Canal found dolomite deposits ideal for natural cement — Rosendale cement, used in the base of the Statue of Liberty and countless 19th-century structures. At its peak, Rosendale's cement industry employed 5,000 workers and produced nearly half of North America's cement. The mine operated until 1970 and is now the only former cement mine in the area open to the public; its unique acoustics have made it a performance venue. Likely named for Jane LeFevre Snyder (1820–1904), who lived at the Century House on the same estate.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Visited through Century House Historical Society tours — check for open mine days or scheduled performance events.<br/>- Pairs naturally with the neighboring Century House Historical Society and Snyder Estate Natural Cement Historic District.<br/><br/>More info: https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=41.8448%2C%20-74.0986]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
