  <Placemark>
    <name>Winslow Homer Studio Scarborough ME</name>
    <description><![CDATA[Winslow Homer Studio<br/><br/>📍 Winslow Homer Road, Prouts Neck, Scarborough, ME<br/><br/>The carriage house Winslow Homer converted into his home and studio in 1884, where he lived and painted his famous marine and crashing-surf coastal landscapes until his death in 1910. Designated a National Historic Landmark in 1965, and now owned and managed by the Portland Museum of Art. Not independently accessible — the studio is preserved specifically to protect its integrity, and can only be seen on a guided tour.<br/><br/>Tips<br/>- Book through the Portland Museum of Art — tours are 2.5 hours, depart by van from the museum in downtown Portland, and require advance reservations. This is not a walk-up or drive-up site.<br/>- Tours include a stop at the museum to see Homer's 1894 painting Weatherbeaten before the van departs, plus a custom soundscape recreating how Homer lived and worked.<br/>- Budget the better part of a morning or afternoon for the full round trip from Portland.<br/>- Combine with the Portland Museum of Art itself, since you're departing from there anyway.]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#c-green</styleUrl>
